---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerFileSample from '../components/AnswerFileSample.astro';
import BootLanes from '../components/BootLanes.astro';
import MenuExample from '../components/MenuExample.astro';
import Status from '../components/Status.astro';
import { checkedOn } from '../data/boot-chain';

const checked = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', { dateStyle: 'long', timeZone: 'UTC' }).format(
  new Date(`${checkedOn}T00:00:00Z`),
);

const repo = 'https://github.com/kyro-pxe/kyro';
const roadmap = `${repo}/blob/main/docs/ROADMAP.md`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Kyro: open-source PXE server for Windows 11"
  description="Kyro is an open-source PXE server for installing Windows 11 over the network. It is in early development and cannot boot a machine yet."
>
  <div class="frame">
    <div class="hero">
      <h1>Install Windows&nbsp;11 <span>over the network.</span></h1>
      <div class="pair pair-top">
        <div class="hero-text">
          <p class="lede">
            Kyro is an open-source PXE server in early development. The goal: you point it at a
            Windows 11 ISO, and a PC that boots from the network gets Windows Setup from it. No
            USB stick, no unpacking the ISO by hand.
          </p>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="button" href={repo}>Source on GitHub</a>
            <a class="text-link" href={roadmap}>Read the roadmap</a>
          </div>
          <Status state="in-progress" block>
            Kyro cannot boot a machine yet. This page describes the plan, and each part says how
            far it is.
          </Status>
        </div>
        <div>
          <BootLanes />
          <p class="small chart-note">
            Compared with the <a href={roadmap}>roadmap</a> on {checked}. If the two differ, the
            roadmap is right.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="section pair pair-top" aria-labelledby="why">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2 id="why">One program instead of a stack you assemble by hand.</h2>
        <p>
          Installing Windows over the network usually means putting DHCP, TFTP, HTTP, iPXE and
          wimboot together yourself, or using a tool that is convenient but not fully open source.
        </p>
        <p>
          Kyro aims to be the convenient option with every part open: one program that you point
          at your Windows 11 ISOs, under a license that lets anyone read, audit and change it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 id="facts">What is planned</h3>
        <dl class="facts" aria-labelledby="facts">
          <div><dt>License</dt><dd>GPL, version 3</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Form</dt><dd>One static binary, written in Go</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Runs on</dt><dd>Linux first, then Docker and Windows</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Boots</dt><dd>x86-64 PCs with UEFI or BIOS firmware</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Installs</dt><dd>Windows 11 from an unmodified ISO</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Builds on</dt><dd>iPXE and wimboot, used unmodified</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section pair" aria-labelledby="profiles">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2 id="profiles">One ISO, any number of setups.</h2>
        <p>
          The usual way to keep several Windows configurations is one ISO per configuration, each
          several gigabytes large and almost identical to the others.
        </p>
        <p>
          Kyro keeps the two apart. An image is a Windows ISO. A profile is an answer file with a
          name, and it is small. The boot menu is meant to offer each image with the profiles that
          fit it, and always once without a profile.
        </p>
        <p>
          <Status state="in-progress">The design is proposed. Nothing of it is built.</Status>
        </p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0002-profiles.md`}>Read the profile design</a></p>
      </div>
      <MenuExample />
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="generator">
    <div class="frame pair">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2 id="generator">Answer files, written in your browser.</h2>
        <p>
          Windows Setup asks for a language, a disk, an account and more. An answer file
          (<code>autounattend.xml</code>) answers in advance, so nobody has to sit in front of the
          installer.
        </p>
        <p>
          This site is going to have a form that writes such files. It runs in the browser only:
          what you type, passwords included, stays on your computer. The page already forbids its
          scripts every network connection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <Status state="planned">The options are written down as a proposal. The form is not built.</Status>
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button button-outline" href="https://github.com/kyro-pxe/website/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-generator-options.md">Read the proposed options</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <AnswerFileSample />
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="frame">
    <section class="section pair pair-top" aria-labelledby="security">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2 id="security">Made for a boot network you control.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          PXE has no authentication, and Kyro cannot change that. The security model says so
          before anything else.
        </p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/security-model.md`}>Read the security model</a></p>
      </div>
      <ul class="points">
        <li>
          <h3>Anyone on the network can answer.</h3>
          <p>
            A booting PC acts on the DHCP answers it gets. Any machine in the same broadcast
            domain can point it at a different boot file.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Give it its own VLAN.</h3>
          <p>
            Kyro belongs on an isolated boot VLAN that contains only Kyro and the machines being
            installed.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Answer files are readable there.</h3>
          <p>
            They can hold passwords, and Kyro is going to hand them to every client that boots.
            Keep long-lived secrets out of them.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(2rem, 1.25rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);
    padding-block-start: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 5vw, 5rem);
  }

  /* The second half of the headline starts its own line where there is room. */
  @media (min-width: 46rem) {
    h1 span {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  h1 span {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .hero-text {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .chart-note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
  }

  .facts {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .facts div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 6.5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .facts dt {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .facts dd {
    color: var(--strong);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .points {
    padding: 0;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--line);
    list-style: none;
  }

  .points li {
    padding-block: var(--space-5);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .points p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
  }
</style>
